        "descriptions": [
          {
            "description": "This data includes focus group scenarios and questions, survey instruments, and focus group transcripts and survey data related to the “Making FIMs Work for Multiple Users: Understanding the Value and Function of Flood Inundation Mapping to Support Decision Support” project. This project was funded by a $195,359 grant from the National Oceanic and Atmospheric Administration (NOAA) (NA23OAR4590359) to advance social science research work about flood inundation mapping (FIM) products. The grant supported a three-year (08-01-2023 to 07-31-2026) mixed-methods social science research study. \nThis social science research sought to help improve the delivery and design of FIM products by providing tested, feasible recommendations for their use with targeted professional and general audiences. Ultimately, the findings contribute to more effective messaging and communication of flood inundation risk and assist communities in better preparation to manage potential flood impacts. The results provide partners with important information about how FIM can be incorporated in messaging, what barriers there are to understanding and using FIMs, and how such information could be used in decision making. The results may also help inform future development and roll out efforts for the FIM at a national level.\n\nThe main research questions included:\n• What FIM products do target areas already rely upon? \n• How do they use those products (what decisions do they support and how do they deliver/message the products)? \n• What are barriers to using the FIM information? \n• What improvements to product design or delivery could remove those barriers?\nTo study these questions, the project team tested and evaluated internally developed NWS templates for IDSS briefings using several FIM maps through a series of focus groups in the partnering River Forecast Centers (RFCs). The focus group scenarios included examples of both static FIMs (including those from the AHPS libraries and NWS Flood Categorial HAND FIM libraries,) and Dynamic FIMs, including River Forecast Center (RFC) FIM and the National Water Model (NWM) FIMs. Additionally, the scenarios included some externally produced FIMs specific to the study areas.\nThis study’s methods included focus groups and surveys with residential and professional audiences in the Mid-Atlantic River Forecast Center and West Gulf River Forecast Center areas. Specifically, we held in-person focus groups with residents in Hershey, PA; Vestal, NY, Angleton, TX, and Houston, TX, and virtual focus groups with professionals in those same areas. These areas were selected because they were part of the first 10% of the U.S. that the new NWM FIM was rolled out. During each focus group session, participants completed a pre-session survey about flood experience, challenges, and demographic information. Then participants were led through a flood-related scenario which included briefing packages, hydrographs, probabilistic and other flood forecast tools for both riverine and flash flood risk, along with FIM maps, including the RFC FIM, NWM FIM, and external FIMs from local agencies/partners. The facilitator asked questions about the types of decisions the users make and what information they would need to support their decision-making processes. Following the focus group discussion, participants completed a post-session survey asking for detailed feedback on the products. Focus groups were recorded, transcribed, and analyzed with inductive coding (using MaxQDA software). Based on the data analysis and discussions with NWS partners, the research team redesigned the three FIM products (CatFIM, RFC FIM, and NWM FIM). These were tested through an online survey that was shared with all the previous professional participants as well as several new areas including professionals in Arkansas, Tennessee, Indiana, Kentucky, Mississippi, and Missouri. \nThe research team, including Rachel Hogan Carr, Dr. Kathryn Semmens, Keri Maxfield, and Patrick Painter (all of Nurture Nature Center), have compiled and shared recommendations to NOAA/NWC about the best ways to use and improve upon these products to communicate flood risk information. The data provided here include the focus group scenarios and questions, the pre and post session survey questions (pre-session surveys were the same for all locations but post-session surveys varied due to the use of different scenarios/products), survey responses and focus group transcripts (anonymized). In addition, the online follow-up survey and responses a",
            "descriptionType": "Abstract"
          }
